"## Summary\n\nNetlab follow-up 35: F22-01, isolation 22's D-120 TCP read timeout, under D-159.\n- **Session:** launched 02:13 EST on 2026-11-04 on #459's roadmap row, and delivered at 02:49.\n- **Model:** codex `gpt-6-astra`.\n- **Size:** 7 commits and 397 owned files, +262,167/−6.\n  - The code is 199 lines in `tests/netlab/`: `stream.py`, `trace_capture.py`, the new `trace_split.py` and its tests, and `reload_trace_test.go`.\n  - The rest is the dated note and 391 evidence files under `docs/poc/G2-followup35-evidence/`.\n- **Checks:**\n  - merge-tree clean on current `main`; prepush-scan clean; owned-check passes.\n  - **The orchestrator reran these** on a scratch tree: current `main` (after #461) with this branch merged in. All pass:\n    - `make -f mk/netlab.mk netlab-lint netlab-unit netlab-integration netlab-build`, the 64 offline script tests included;\n    - `make -f mk/at-isolation.mk at-isolation-lint at-isolation-test`, since isolation's assembled run includes this lab;\n    - `go vet -tags netlab ./tests/netlab/...`;\n    - `go test ./tests/handover/...`, and the committed-evidence tests in `tests/netlab`, `tests/e2e/at/isolation` and `tests/e2e`;\n    - `make go-lint`, gofmt and `git diff --check`.\n- **Scope:** no production, consumer, dependency or pin change. Each attempt ran in the lane's own prefix, with no reservation. Zero owned Docker objects at the end.\n\n**The orchestrator's reading.**\n- **The diagnosis: timing-compatible, not proven.** Both F22-01 (sequence 101) and F27-01/B1 (sequence 43) fit one picture: a dropped TCP segment whose retries hit later guards at the campaign's cadence.\n  - **Follow-up 30's measured retry offsets,** +208 and +416 ms, transplanted onto each historical send, land in F22-01's generation 1091 and 1092 and B1's 1066 and 1068 install brackets.\n  - **Follow-up 28's cycle 40** measured a third retry at +826 ms. It lands in F22-01's generation 1094 and B1's 1072 install brackets.\n  - **Neither run retained TCP packets,** so the attribution stays open.\n- **D-159's condition is false: the agent has no positive minimum Apply spacing.**\n  - Three paths can apply within 400 ms: binding-deadline wake-ups (`node/agent/engine.go:647`), a zero remaining Step delay (`serving.go:20`), and a closed recovery's second Apply in the same Step (`engine.go:785` → `sync.go:256`).\n  - So the campaign's cadence is unchanged.\n- **The evidence additions.**\n  - **The captures:** the three collectors, origin, node and responder, now capture `udp port 9000 or tcp port 9001`.\n    - They keep headers only, with absolute TCP sequence numbers.\n    - Each is retained as `at10-<role>-headers.txt`, with tcpdump's own counters.\n  - **The UDP input:** `trace_split.py` derives it for the unchanged analyzer. It first rejects any unparsed TCP or UDP packet, a count mismatch or a kernel drop.\n  - **The stream:** `stream.py` records each TCP read's echo as match, EOF, stale or mismatch, with a derived test-only sequence identity. This is observational: no verdict changes.\n  - **Unchanged:** `classify_d120`, the campaign loop and every D-120 predicate are byte-identical.\n- **Five standalone attempts and the full lab.**\n  - **Attempt 1** completed its reloads, then the new splitter rejected tcpdump's trailing blank separator. It stays failed. The fix keeps packet counts, and has a regression test.\n  - **Attempts 2 to 5,** on the corrected tree, passed with zero TCP timeouts. TCP p95 was 205 to 209 ms in each.\n  - **Attempts 3 to 5 are conservatively confounded.** A matching foreign process appeared in their samples: pid 2454422, `bash`, 0 ticks.\n  - **Attempt 3, sequence 47:**\n    - the same four bytes left the origin at +0.06, +205.9 and +611.9 ms;\n    - the first two copies entered the node inside generations 1069's and 1070's install brackets, and were not captured leaving it;\n    - the read completed at 613.2 ms.\n    This is header-path evidence, not an NFLOG attribution.\n  - **The full netlab gate** passed in 247 s, with 101 verified Applies and D-120 passing.\n  - **The consumers:** all 13 agent scenarios pass, and so do the node and installer consumers and isolation's lint and test.\n- **The lane's requests,** answered in netlab 36's brief:\n  - **no agent-core change:** the sub-400 ms paths are product behaviour;\n  - **the operator sentence** goes to handover's next follow-up as written;\n  - **the attempt count:** attempt 1 counts among the five and stays failed, and no sixth attempt is needed;\n  - **nested admission:** nested Make and Docker invocations inside an admitted command need no admission of their own.\n- **D-161** (orchestrator, provisional; the owner may overturn) replaces D-120's \"zero TCP timeouts\". Every other D-120 predicate stands.\n  - **The rule:** a TCP read timeout is a guard loss, not a failure, only if the run's complete, checked captures show all of these:\n    - every lost transmission of the stalled bytes entered the node inside exactly one verified guard bracket, within both 100 ms bounds, and never left it;\n    - a later transmission delivered the bytes in order on the same connection;\n    - the connection was never reset or closed.\n  - **The count:** guard-attributed timeouts count toward \"≤ 5 losses per 100 reloads\".\n  - **What stays failed:** historical failures, which have no TCP captures.\n  - **Why:**\n    - D-145 keeps the guard;\n    - TCP recovers a dropped segment only by retransmission, so a retry can land in a later reload's guard, as in sequence 47;\n    - D-120 is a test-acceptance definition, not a product guarantee.\n  - **Next:** netlab 36 implements it in the analyzer. It is armed on this merge.\n"
